1. AGGREGATE BENCHMARK — better than what alternative?
==============================================================================
  note: REGION contains ['CZ'], absent from this ESS edition; ignored
  bloc assignment written to country_bloc_assignment.csv (required for reproducibility; Israel is a bloc of one and therefore falls back to the all-other-countries mean)


  predictor                                                 median r  median nMAE
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  the model, as produced                                     +0.4433       0.1796
  the model, per-item bias removed (UPPER BOUND, needs truth)   +0.4433       0.0644
  leave-one-out region mean (no model)                       +0.5542       0.0559
  grand mean, IN-SAMPLE (no model, cannot rank at all)       +0.0000       0.0715
  grand mean, leave-one-out (matched to the region arm)         +nan       0.0740

  WARNING: undefined correlations by predictor: {'model': 0, 'debias': 0, 'region': 0, 'grand': 0, 'grand_loo': 42}
  Head-to-head counts below exclude those items; investigate before
  quoting the counts.

  head-to-head, item by item (out of 42):
    model beats leave-one-out region mean                    on r:  18   on nMAE:  1
    debiased model beats                                       on nMAE: 13
    model beats grand mean, IN-SAMPLE                        on r:  31   on nMAE:  2
    debiased model beats                                       on nMAE: 30
    model beats grand mean, leave-one-out                    on r:   n/a   on nMAE:  3
    debiased model beats                                       on nMAE: 30

  Removing a single per-item offset cuts the model's error by:
     64.2%   ratio of medians (as released)
     62.9%   ratio of means
     59.9%   MEDIAN of the per-item reductions (recommended)
     53.7%   mean of the per-item reductions
  Report 59.9% and name the estimator. Quoting the ratio of
  medians without naming it invites a reader who recomputes per item to
  get a figure five points lower.
  That is the quantitative form of 'the levels are displaced but the
  country-to-country pattern is largely intact'. It also means the
  headline error is dominated by a constant, not by country-specific
  mistakes — which is what a group-level prior with a fixed offset
  would produce, and is consistent with the compression and bias
  results reported elsewhere.

  IMPORTANT FRAMING: the region and grand-mean comparators both
  require survey data from other countries. State that explicitly.
  The comparison answers 'should a researcher who already holds real
  data use the model to extend it', which is the realistic use case,
  not 'is the model better than nothing'.

